'''United Future New Zealand''',<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/UnitedFuture_Constitution.pdf |title=Constitution of United Future New Zealand |access-date=8 June 2013 |quote=The name of the party shall be United Future New Zealand |archive-date=6 October 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075522/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/UnitedFuture_Constitution.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> usually known as '''United Future''', was a [[Centrism|centrist]] [[List of political parties in New Zealand|political party in New Zealand]]. The party was in government between 2005 and 2017, first alongside [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] (2005–2008) and then supporting [[New Zealand National Party|National]] (2008–2017).

United Future was formed from the merger of the liberal party [[United New Zealand]] and Christian-dominated conservative [[Future New Zealand]] to contest the [[2002 New Zealand general election|2002 election]]. It was represented in the [[New Zealand Parliament]] from its foundation until September 2017. The party won eight seats in 2002; however it was reduced to three [[Members of Parliament]] in [[2005 New Zealand general election|2005]]. Between [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008]] and [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017]], United Future was solely represented in Parliament by party leader [[Peter Dunne]], who represented the [[Ōhāriu (New Zealand electorate)|Ōhāriu electorate]] in [[Wellington]]. Dunne was re-elected during both the [[2011 New Zealand general election|2011]] and [[2014 New Zealand general election|2014 general election]]s.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/parties/ |title=New Zealand Parliament - Parliamentary parties |access-date=2013-08-13 |url-status=dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20061012123234/http://www.parliament.nz/en-NZ/MPP/Parties/ |archive-date=12 October 2006}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/mps/current/ |title=Members of Parliament - New Zealand Parliament |website=Parliament.nz |access-date=2017-04-10 |archive-date=7 October 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141007015457/http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/mps/current |url-status=live }}</ref>

In August 2017, Dunne announced his retirement from politics prior to the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]]. [[Damian Light]] was appointed as the new leader on 23 August.<ref name="leader">{{cite web|title=UnitedFuture appoints Damian Light as new leader|url=http://unitedfuture.org.nz/unitedfuture-appoints-damian-light-as-new-leader/|publisher=United Future New Zealand|access-date=23 August 2017|language=en|date=24 August 2017|archive-date=21 October 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171021034200/http://unitedfuture.org.nz/unitedfuture-appoints-damian-light-as-new-leader/|url-status=live}}</ref> During the 2017 general election, United Future lost its sole seat in Parliament and attained only 0.1% of the party vote.<ref name="2017 general election">{{cite web|title=2017 General Election - Official Result|url=http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2017/|publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|New Zealand Electoral Commission]]|access-date=7 October 2017|archive-date=7 October 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171007120051/http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2017/|url-status=live}}</ref>

In November 2017, a leaked email announced that United Future would move to dissolve after a unanimous decision by the party board to do so. On 14 November an announcement was made on the party's website signalling that the party had officially been dissolved.<ref name="Dissolution statement">
